  "_scope_comment": [
    "Scope cases test a rule that lives ABOVE a single subrequirement: how far",
    "does 'a course can only count once' reach?",
    "",
    "A real UC San Diego audit double-counts ACROSS categories but not within",
    "one. In the reference audit, BILD 1 and BILD 3 each satisfy Breadth/Natural",
    "Science AND Major/Subject Domain; PHIL 35 satisfies Breadth/Humanities AND",
    "UC Writing/DEI. So exclusivity is per audit section, not global.",
    "",
    "This was a real drift: the sidebar scoped it per section while the agent",
    "scoped it globally, so the agent told students to add a course they had",
    "already planned. The per-subrequirement cases above cannot catch it,",
    "because they only ever describe one subrequirement.",
    "",
    "Each side maps these onto its own entry point (evaluateRequirement per",
    "section in JS, check_coverage over all sections in Python) and reports the",
    "set of SATISFIED subrequirement titles.",
    "",
    "Rows that state no NEEDS at all are SKIPPED by both sides - they are not",
    "reported satisfied and they do not block anything. A row with an options",
    "list but no parsed NEEDS is such a row."
  ],
